      <h1>Leonardo da Vinci</h1>
      <p class="report-dek">
        Not only "artist and inventor." In a modern frame he is one of the first people to
        treat looking as a scientific instrument — and to paint as if the air itself were part
        of the picture.
      </p>
      <p class="report-byline">Artometrics Glossary · sample entry · scaffold draft</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        If you look Leonardo up in a general encyclopedia, you get the résumé: Florentine
        polymath, painter, engineer, the prototype Renaissance man. That is true and not very
        useful. The thing that still matters is the method. He treated the body, the landscape,
        and motion as problems you could draw your way through. Anatomy as it is practiced now
        — looking inside the body to understand how it works — is already there in the notebooks.
        The secret, if you want one word for it, is water. He watched how water curls, eddies,
        braids, and breaks, and he used that study as a master key for hair, drapery, weather,
        and the way living forms move.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why the paintings feel ahead of their time. At the height of the Renaissance he
        could draw with a precision people still call scientific, and then paint with a
        controlled messiness — almost like smoke — that let edges dissolve the way they do in
        actual air and actual light. The technique later called sfumato is the visible form of
        that idea. It is as close as a hand-made picture could get to a photograph before
        photography was invented: not because it is tight and linear, but because it captures
        the atmosphere sitting between you and the face.
      </p>
      <p>
        The career on paper is smaller than the legend. He finished relatively few paintings.
        Much of what we think we "know" is reconstructed from the notebooks, from later
        biographers, and from a thin set of contemporary notices. There is not a large body of
        first-person written account in the way a modern life would leave one. That scarcity is
        part of the entry, not a footnote. It is why the work, the drawings, and the surviving
        pictures have to carry more of the biography than the prose record can.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read that way, Leonardo is less a remote genius and more a model for the art-scientist
        (or science-artist) this whole project is for: someone who would not separate close
        looking from experiment, and who let a study of the physical world change how the
        picture was made. For the visual language itself, see the style board on
        <a href="/styles/sfumato">sfumato</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="sources" aria-labelledby="sources-title">
      <h2 id="sources-title">Sources</h2>
      <p>
        This is a scaffold draft, not a finished sourced biography. A full entry should lean on
        the primary notebooks (the surviving manuscript corpus, including the studies of water
        and anatomy), the small finished painted oeuvre, and the early biographical tradition
        — especially Giorgio Vasari's <em>Lives</em>, which is influential and not always
        reliable. Most popular accounts compress those layers into a clean story. The honest
        version should keep the seams visible: what comes from Leonardo's own hand, what comes
        from later narrative, and what we simply do not have.
      </p>
    </section>
